  <div class="page wrap prose">
    <h1>About this directory</h1>
    <h2>How a listing is checked</h2>
    <p>Charlotte is the first city. The churches here were checked against their own websites. The listing keeps the service times in the church’s words, a short quote, the page that quote came from, and the date of the check.</p>
    <h2>Open and closed</h2>
    <p>A listing is either open or closed. Closing a church does not delete the row. It drops off the map and the public lists, and the page says it is marked closed.</p>
    <p>Nothing on the public site changes because someone filled out a form. A submission, a suggested edit, or a listing check sits in a review queue until it is approved.</p>
    <h2>What we do not guess</h2>
    <p>Church type is the plain name, such as Baptist or Catholic. When a church names a narrower group, such as a convention or diocese, that line sits under the name as "Part of ...". If the pages we read did not say, the line is left off. That is not a no.</p>
    <p>Some bodies are tagged as a historically Black tradition. That tag comes from the denomination, not from a guess about who attends.</p>
    <p>Kids programs, worship style, theology, young adult ministry, LGBTQ stance, and outreach are columns in the database. They are empty. We will fill one only when the church states it. We do not infer a stance.</p>
    <h2>How to fix a listing</h2>
    <p>If a time, address, or phone is wrong, use Suggest a correction on the church page. If a church is missing, use <a href="/submit">Add a church</a>.</p>
    <h2>The photograph</h2>
    <p>The picture across the top of the site was made for Find a Church. It shows a country church at sunrise. It is not a photograph of a congregation in this directory.</p>
  </div>
</Layout>
